<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🧊 LIVE DEW POINT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/dew_point/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t17z-dpt-anl-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Dew-point temperature in degrees Fahrenheit (HRRR 2 m analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST -20</b> deep-purple very dry &rarr; blue &rarr; teal at <b>freezing 32</b> &rarr; green &rarr; yellow humid &rarr; amber muggy &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 90</b> red oppressive. Same dew point always shows the same color. Actual dew-point temperature — not spread, not probability, not relative humidity.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m dew point analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE DEW POINT</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/dew_point/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t17z-dpt-anl-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Dew-point temperature in degrees Fahrenheit (HRRR 2 m analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST -20</b> deep-purple very dry &rarr; blue &rarr; teal at <b>freezing 32</b> &rarr; green &rarr; yellow humid &rarr; amber muggy &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 90</b> red oppressive. Same dew point always shows the same color. Actual dew-point temperature — not spread, not probability, not relative humidity.</p><p>Near-surface (2 m) dew-point temperature from the operational HRRR analysis: the temperature to which air must cool for saturation. High dew points mark muggy, moisture-laden air masses over the lakes; low dew points mark dry continental air. Displayed in degrees Fahrenheit like the project's air-temperature layer.</p><p>Dew-point science (atmospheric thermodynamics) treats dew point as the absolute moisture variable: unlike relative humidity it does not shift with temperature alone, so it tracks air-mass moisture through day and night. It sets condensation onset, fog potential, and human heat stress, and is reported here as an actual temperature in degrees Fahrenheit.</p><p><b>Units:</b> degF (display; source K)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m dew point analysis, hourly cycles<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>🧊 LIVE DEW POINT</name>
        <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
        <Icon>
          <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/dew_point/current.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t17z-dpt-anl-g2</href>
          <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
          <refreshInterval>3600</refreshInterval>
        </Icon>
        <LatLonBox>
          <north>49.5</north>
          <south>40.5</south>
          <east>-73.5</east>
          <west>-93.0</west>
        </LatLonBox>
      </GroundOverlay>
    </Folder>
  </Document>
</kml>
